 ### [Primitive Geometry and Distribution](6228-primitive-geometry-and-distribution.md)
-This family defines the geometric primitives used to represent 3D scenes in differentiable splatting methods, extending the standard 3D Gaussian ellipsoid to alternative shapes, distributions, and dimensionalities. It solves the limitations of volumetric Gaussian mixtures, such as ambiguous geometry, poor mesh extraction, and inefficient representation of sharp edges or opaque surfaces, by introducing planar disks, fat-tailed distributions, generalized exponential functions, and polyhedral volumes. It differs from sibling methods by varying the fundamental density function or geometric support of the primitive while maintaining the core splatting pipeline of projection and alpha compositing. Choose it when the standard Gaussian density function fails to capture sharp edges or specific surface topologies. See [Primitive Geometry and Distribution](6228-primitive-geometry-and-distribution.md).
+This family defines the geometric primitives used to represent 3D scenes in differentiable splatting methods, extending the standard 3D Gaussian ellipsoid to alternative shapes, distributions, and dimensionalities [G]. It addresses specific limitations of standard volumetric representations by introducing specialized primitives: planar disks for surface reconstruction [2D Gaussian Splatting](6068-2d-gaussian-splatting.md), fat-tailed distributions for non-monotonic density modeling [3D Student's t Distribution Splatting](4352-3d-student-s-t-distribution-splatting.md), generalized exponential functions for high-frequency content [Generalized Exponential Splatting](4684-generalized-exponential-splatting.md), and transparent polyhedra for linear volumetric rendering [LinPrim Octahedron Representation]. It differs from sibling methods by varying the fundamental density function or geometric support of the primitive while maintaining the core splatting pipeline of projection and alpha compositing [G].
 
 ### [3D Half-Gaussian Splatting](9011-3d-half-gaussian-splatting.md)
-3D Half-Gaussian Splatting (3D-HGS) is a reconstruction kernel for 3D Gaussian Splatting that replaces the standard full 3D Gaussian with a 3D Half-Gaussian to address shape and color discontinuities (Gibbs phenomenon) at object boundaries and texture-rich areas. It achieves higher rendering quality without compromising speed by allowing each half of the split Gaussian to have different opacity values while sharing geometric parameters. Unlike 2D-GS, which performs surface rendering, 3D-HGS retains volumetric rendering to preserve performance while enabling accurate modeling of discontinuous functions. Choose it when volumetric rendering must handle sharp boundaries and discontinuities without switching to surface-based methods. See [3D Half-Gaussian Splatting](9011-3d-half-gaussian-splatting.md).
+3D Half-Gaussian Splatting (3D-HGS) is a reconstruction kernel for 3D Gaussian Splatting that replaces the standard full 3D Gaussian with a 3D Half-Gaussian to address shape and color discontinuities (Gibbs phenomenon) at object boundaries and texture-rich areas. It achieves higher rendering quality without compromising speed by allowing each half of the split Gaussian to have different opacity values while sharing geometric parameters. Unlike 2D-GS, which performs surface rendering, 3D-HGS retains volumetric rendering to preserve performance while enabling accurate modeling of discontinuous functions.
 
 ### [Texture and Appearance Representation](9528-texture-and-appearance-representation.md)
-Texture and Appearance Representation defines how the color $c_i$ of a 3D Gaussian primitive is determined during rendering, extending the basic geometric parameters ($\boldsymbol{\mu}_i, \boldsymbol{\Sigma}_i$) and opacity $\alpha_i$. It solves the limitation of static color storage by enabling high-frequency details or view-dependent effects (specular highlights, anisotropy) without excessively increasing the number of primitives. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like [GaussianSet Data Structure](2074-gaussianset-data-structure.md) by focusing on the semantic definition of color rather than memory layout, and from [Primitive Geometry and Distribution](6228-primitive-geometry-and-distribution.md) by addressing radiance rather than shape [G]. Choose it when view-dependent shading or high-frequency texture mapping is required. See [Texture and Appearance Representation](9528-texture-and-appearance-representation.md).
+Texture and Appearance Representation covers methods that extend 3D Gaussian primitives with appearance attributes to determine color during rendering [G]. It solves the limitation of static color storage by enabling high-frequency details or view-dependent effects without excessively increasing the number of primitives. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like [GaussianSet Data Structure](2074-gaussianset-data-structure.md) by focusing on the semantic definition of color rather than memory layout, and from [Primitive Geometry and Distribution](6228-primitive-geometry-and-distribution.md) by addressing radiance rather than shape [G].
 
 ### [Bézier Gaussian Triangle](11816-b-zier-gaussian-triangle.md)
-The Bézier Gaussian Triangle (BG-Triangle) is a hybrid 3D primitive representation that combines Bézier triangle surfaces for explicit geometry with 3D Gaussian sub-primitives for appearance, enabling differentiable rendering with sharp boundaries. It solves the problem of blurry or aliased edges common in pure Gaussian splatting by defining the object's silhouette and surface topology via parametric Bézier patches, while using Gaussians only for shading and color. Unlike standard 3D Gaussian Splatting, which relies on the overlap of many opaque Gaussians to form shapes, BG-Triangle maintains distinct, sharp boundaries between primitives, making it particularly suitable for vectorization and compact scene representation. Choose it when explicit, sharp geometric boundaries and vectorization are prioritized over pure volumetric blending. See [Bézier Gaussian Triangle](11816-b-zier-gaussian-triangle.md).
