 ### Negative Component Splatting
-A technique using negative density components in a mixture model to enhance representational power and parameter efficiency, allowing subtraction of color/density to capture complex topologies (e.g., holes) with fewer components [S2]. The rendering equation uses signed weights $w \in \mathbb{R}$ (instead of $w > 0$) in the standard alpha-compositing sum: $C(u) = \sum_{i=1}^N c_i o_i T_i^{2D}(u) \prod_{j=1}^{i-1} (1 - o_j T_j^{2D}(u))$, where $o_i$ is opacity and $T_i^{2D}$ is the 2D component function [S2]. Opacity $o$ is constrained by a tanh function during learning so that $o \in [-1, 1]$, allowing positive and negative components to dynamically change signs while being bounded [S2]. Using squared negative components introduces pairwise interactions between all $K$ components, increasing evaluation complexity to $O(n^2)$, which is significantly slower; therefore, the linear sum with signed weights is used instead [S2]. Choose it when capturing complex topologies like holes requires fewer components than positive-only mixtures.
