 ## Background
-The concept of representing scenes with explicit geometric primitives for differentiable rendering emerged from the need to combine the speed of rasterization with the flexibility of neural fields [G]. The standard 3D Gaussian Splatting approach [Parent] popularized the use of anisotropic 3D Gaussians as the primary primitive [G]. Subsequent research explored alternative primitives to address specific limitations such as sharp edges (Bézier Gaussians), surface accuracy (Surfels, Half-Gaussians), and computational efficiency (Conditional Formulations) [G]. The family of Primitive Representations continues to evolve to support diverse applications ranging from static reconstruction to dynamic scenes and texture mapping [G].
+The concept of primitive representation in volume rendering has evolved from simple geometric primitives (points, lines, triangles) to probabilistic distributions (Gaussians, t-distributions) to enable differentiable rendering. The standard 3D Gaussian primitive, popularized by 3D Gaussian Splatting (Kerbl et al., 2023), provides a balance between representational capacity and computational efficiency [G]. Subsequent research has explored alternative distributions (e.g., Student's t, Half-Gaussians) and dimensionalities (2D surfels) to address specific limitations such as aliasing, surface reconstruction accuracy, and handling of discontinuities [G]. The field continues to expand with hybrid representations (e.g., Bézier-Gaussian) and advanced data structures to optimize training and rendering performance [G].
 
